From db6cd091a9b1d6fa369a3b182e4ac4882544624f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Seuj09 <jeus110310@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 06:17:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: sprd_wdt: keep a watchdog the bootloader started
 fed

The probe enables the clocks and registers the device, but only start()
marks the watchdog as running. A counter the bootloader had already
started therefore looks stopped to the watchdog core, which does not feed
it before userspace opens /dev/watchdog, and the SoC resets after the
configured timeout (12 seconds on the UMS9230 board trees) while nothing
has run that could open it.

Look at the counter enable bit once the clocks are on and, if it is set,
report the watchdog as running so the core keeps it fed until userspace
takes over.

Signed-off-by: Seuj09 <jeus110310@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt.c
index aacf04616..7d6731d46 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt.c
@@ -310,6 +310,16 @@ static int sprd_wdt_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		dev_err(dev, "failed to enable wdt\n");
 		return ret;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The bootloader may already have started the counter. The core only
+	 * keeps a running watchdog fed until userspace opens it if it is told
+	 * so, and start() is the one place that sets the flag, so a counter
+	 * left running would otherwise expire and reset the system before
+	 * anything had a chance to open it.
+	 */
+	if (readl_relaxed(wdt->base + SPRD_WDT_CTRL) & SPRD_WDT_CNT_EN_BIT)
+		set_bit(WDOG_HW_RUNNING, &wdt->wdd.status);
 	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, sprd_wdt_disable, wdt);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "Failed to add wdt disable action\n");
-- 
